---
import SquarespaceFooter from '../components/SquarespaceFooter.astro';
import SquarespaceHeader from '../components/SquarespaceHeader.astro';
import type { ShellConfig } from '../config/shell';
import { shellConfig } from '../config/shell';
import SquarespaceShellLayout from '../layouts/SquarespaceShellLayout.astro';

const { config = shellConfig }: { config?: ShellConfig } = Astro.props;
---
<SquarespaceShellLayout config={config}>
  <div id="siteWrapper" class="clearfix site-wrapper">
    <SquarespaceHeader config={config} />

    <main id="page" class="ht-main" tabindex="-1">

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 1 — HERO
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-sat-hero">
        <div class="ht-sat-hero__inner">
          <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--crimson">Your comprehensive guide</p>
          <h1 class="ht-sat-hero__h1">The SAT</h1>
          <p class="ht-sat-hero__lead">
            I've been teaching the SAT since 2007. Like the ACT, the SAT
            can be learned — because it is made up of only so many types of
            questions. Learn to recognize them, learn the steps to answer
            each one, and the exam becomes a mechanical process.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 2 — GUIDE BODY (sticky TOC + content)
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-sat-guide">
        <div class="ht-sat-guide__inner">

          <!-- LEFT — STICKY TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
          <nav class="ht-sat-toc" aria-label="Page sections">
            <span class="ht-sat-toc__heading">On this page</span>
            <ul class="ht-sat-toc__list">
              <li class="ht-sat-toc__item">
                <a href="/sat#sat-intro" class="ht-sat-toc__link">1 — Introduction</a>
              </li>
              <li class="ht-sat-toc__item">
                <a href="/sat#sat-rw" class="ht-sat-toc__link">2 — Reading &amp; Writing</a>
              </li>
              <li class="ht-sat-toc__item">
                <a href="/sat#sat-math" class="ht-sat-toc__link">3 — Math</a>
              </li>
              <li class="ht-sat-toc__item">
                <a href="/sat#sat-conclusion" class="ht-sat-toc__link">4 — Conclusion</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </nav>

          <!-- RIGHT — GUIDE CONTENT -->
          <div class="ht-sat-content">

            <!-- ── Section 1: Introduction ── -->
            <div id="sat-intro" class="ht-sat-section">
              <span class="ht-sat-section__label">Introduction</span>
              <h2 class="ht-sat-section__h2">The SAT can be learned</h2>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                I've been teaching the SAT since 2007. Like the ACT, or rather
                like most standardized tests, the SAT CAN BE LEARNED. The key is
                to recognize that the exam is made up of only so many types of
                questions. The best way to increase your score is to learn the
                types of questions, how to recognize them, and the steps to get
                to the right answer. You can literally learn to become an SAT
                robot.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Since March of 2024, the SAT has adopted a new, digital format.
                The test is now conducted on an electronic device and is adaptive.
                The new format has two scores: Reading/Writing and Math. Each
                score is tested over two modules, for a total of four modules.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Verbal Modules 1 and 2</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Both modules have the same format. Each consists of 27 questions
                and 32 minutes. The first set of approximately six questions cover
                vocabulary, the next cover logical reasoning, then grammar,
                punctuation, and standards of English, and finally synthesizing
                information — which asks which answer fulfills a writer's goal,
                given a set of notes.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Math Modules 1 and 2</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Both modules have the same format. Each consists of 22 questions
                and 35 minutes. The two Math modules cover a rather small range
                of math topics, requiring nothing more than knowledge covered in
                Algebra II — unlike the ACT, which covers a much broader array
                of information. However, this can be deceptive. Though they
                require very little math knowledge, the SAT math modules can
                require very high levels of analytical ability. Some would call
                the questions 'tricky.'
              </p>
            </div><!-- /#sat-intro -->

            <!-- ── Section 2: Reading & Writing ── -->
            <div id="sat-rw" class="ht-sat-section">
              <span class="ht-sat-section__label">Section 2</span>
              <h2 class="ht-sat-section__h2">Reading &amp; Writing</h2>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Many people consider the SAT Reading and Writing section to be
                very 'tricky,' and I would agree with them for the most part.
                However, tricky doesn't necessarily mean hard — at least not if
                you know the methods the College Board is using to make it hard.
                Like anything on the SAT, the best and easiest way to conquer
                this section is to learn the question types, how to recognize
                them, and the steps to take to get to the right answer. Turn it
                into a process. The less thinking, the better.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Vocabulary</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                The first set of questions — about 5 or 6 — are standard
                vocabulary questions. Students are given a prompt and must select
                an appropriate word given the context of the text.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Logical Reasoning</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                The next set of questions, up to about 17 or 18, essentially
                test logical reasoning: support a claim, weaken a claim, provide
                a logical conclusion, identify the conclusion.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Conventions of English</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                The final 8 or 9 questions cover standard conventions of
                English: grammar, punctuation, verb tenses, modifiers,
                run-ons, and so on.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">Synthesizing Information</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                You are provided a bullet list of notes a 'student' has taken
                on a subject, and then asked which answer would satisfy the
                student's goal. For example: a student wants to identify two
                activists in the civil rights movement and their role in it.
                Which answer would fulfill this goal?
              </p>
            </div><!-- /#sat-rw -->

            <!-- ── Section 3: Math ── -->
            <div id="sat-math" class="ht-sat-section">
              <span class="ht-sat-section__label">Section 3</span>
              <h2 class="ht-sat-section__h2">Math</h2>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                If there's one thing I can convince you of about the SAT Math
                section, it's that learning math the way you would in school is
                not the most effective way to increase your score. Learning some
                math is necessary, but better than that is to learn the question
                types and the test-taking strategies.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">1. Math Knowledge</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                What math do you need to know? Arithmetic, algebra and
                functions, geometry, numbers and operations, statistics and
                probability. Nothing beyond what you've learned in Algebra II.
                Within each of these topics, the actual questions are very
                narrow. You can learn what you need in a short amount of time —
                and I only teach what's on the exam.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                As for geometry formulas and equations, the SAT provides them
                at the beginning of each math section. That said, I strongly
                recommend knowing them by heart beforehand — you don't want to
                waste time looking them up during the exam.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">2. Question Types</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                I can never stress this enough: learning that the test is
                composed of only so many different types of questions is
                invaluable. These types are tested over and over again. Learn
                the types of questions, how to recognize them, and the steps to
                answer them — and you'll find the SAT reduced to a simple
                mechanical process. Like learning to hit a golf ball, throw a
                pitch, or execute a tennis serve, it's just a few easy steps
                that become second nature.
              </p>

              <h3 class="ht-sat-section__subh">3. Test-Taking Strategies</h3>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                There are two major strategies that are extremely effective,
                and both are driven by this overall maxim: always seek to turn
                the abstract into concrete. If the answer choices all contain
                variables, substitute your own number for that variable. If a
                question asks you to perform operations on a value without
                telling you what it is, make one up. If the answers are all
                numbers, use those numbers instead of doing the math — plug in
                answer choices until one works.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Learn how to be a good test taker instead of a good math
                student. There's a difference.
              </p>
            </div><!-- /#sat-math -->

            <!-- ── Section 4: Conclusion ── -->
            <div id="sat-conclusion" class="ht-sat-section ht-sat-section--last">
              <span class="ht-sat-section__label">Conclusion</span>
              <h2 class="ht-sat-section__h2">This test is learnable — by anyone</h2>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                If you're not tired of hearing this already: this test is
                learnable, and the best method to improvement is learning
                question types. There are only so many different types of
                questions that might show up on the SAT. If you learn them, how
                to recognize them, and the steps to answer them, you can conquer
                this exam.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                Now, please don't let me mislead you. Improving on the SAT is
                simple, but it may not always be easy. I can teach you exactly
                how to do it, but practice is certainly required — as is time.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                I've taught hundreds of students using my methods. I know what
                works and what doesn't, which is why I've been able to maintain
                my business using strictly word of mouth. Ideally, you should
                allow 2 months of preparation: one 1.5-hour session and two
                hours of homework per week.
              </p>

              <p class="ht-sat-section__p">
                If you have any questions about my services, want some testing
                materials, or just want some questions answered, please feel
                free to drop me a line. Email or call anytime.
              </p>

              <!-- CTA box -->
              <div class="ht-sat-cta-box">
                <p class="ht-sat-cta-box__p">
                  Ready to get started? I'll provide a free practice SAT,
                  answer any questions, and give you honest advice — whether
                  that means working with me or not.
                </p>
                <div class="ht-sat-cta-box__links">
                  <a href="/contact" class="ht-btn ht-btn--primary">Get in touch</a>
                  <a href="/faq" class="ht-btn ht-btn--secondary">Read the FAQs</a>
                </div>
              </div>

            </div><!-- /#sat-conclusion -->

          </div><!-- /.ht-sat-content -->
        </div><!-- /.ht-sat-guide__inner -->
      </section>

    </main>

    <SquarespaceFooter config={config} />
  </div>

  <script is:inline defer="true" src={config.siteBundle} type="text/javascript"></script>
  <div id="yui3-css-stamp" style="position: absolute !important; visibility: hidden !important"></div>
</SquarespaceShellLayout>

<style>
  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     Smooth scroll & section offset for sticky header
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  :global(html) {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     Reset Squarespace container constraints on main
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  :global(#page.ht-main) {
    max-width: none !important;
    padding: 0 !important;
    margin: 0 !important;
    width: 100% !important;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     Shared utilities
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-widest);
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  .ht-eyebrow--crimson {
    color: var(--color-accent-crimson);
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     SECTION 1 — HERO
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-sat-hero {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding-top: clamp(12rem, 16vw, 15rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    padding-left: var(--space-section-h);
    padding-right: var(--space-section-h);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .ht-sat-hero__inner {
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .ht-sat-hero__h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 2.75rem);
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }

  .ht-sat-hero__lead {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     SECTION 2 — GUIDE BODY
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-sat-guide {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem) var(--space-section-h)
             clamp(6rem, 10vw, 9rem) var(--space-section-h);
  }

  .ht-sat-guide__inner {
    max-width: var(--content-full);
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    gap: clamp(3rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     LEFT — STICKY TOC
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-sat-toc {
    flex: 0 0 220px;
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(143px + 2rem);
    align-self: flex-start;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--color-surface-warm);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-widest);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    display: block;
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__item {
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__link {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border-left: 2px solid transparent;
    transition: all 150ms ease;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__link:hover {
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .ht-sat-toc__link--active {
    color: var(--color-accent-crimson);
    border-left-color: var(--color-accent-crimson);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     RIGHT — GUIDE CONTENT
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-sat-content {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Individual content sections */
  .ht-sat-section {
    padding-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    scroll-margin-top: calc(143px + 2rem);
  }

  .ht-sat-section--last {
    border-bottom: none;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .ht-sat-section__label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-widest);
    color: var(--color-accent-gold);
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .ht-sat-section__h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .ht-sat-section__p {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .ht-sat-section__p:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .ht-sat-section__subh {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     CTA BOX (inside conclusion section)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-sat-cta-box {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    background: var(--color-surface-warm);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent-gold);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
  }

  .ht-sat-cta-box__p {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .ht-sat-cta-box__links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     BUTTONS
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ht-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    border-radius: var(--radius-button);
    padding: 0.85em 1.75em;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    white-space: nowrap;
    line-height: 1;
    transition: background var(--transition-fast), color var(--transition-fast),
                border-color var(--transition-fast), opacity var(--transition-fast);
    border: none;
  }

  .ht-btn--primary {
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-light);
  }

  .ht-btn--primary:hover {
    background: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .ht-btn--secondary {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--color-text-dark);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-border-strong);
  }

  .ht-btn--secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-text-dark);
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
     Responsive
  ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .ht-sat-guide__inner {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .ht-sat-toc {
      flex: none;
      width: 100%;
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Sticky TOC — active state via IntersectionObserver
  // ----------------------------------------------------------------
  (function () {
    const sections = document.querySelectorAll('.ht-sat-section');
    const tocLinks = document.querySelectorAll('.ht-sat-toc__link');

    if (!sections.length || !tocLinks.length) return;

    function setActive(id) {
      tocLinks.forEach(function (link) {
        if (link.getAttribute('href') === '/sat#' + id) {
          link.classList.add('ht-sat-toc__link--active');
        } else {
          link.classList.remove('ht-sat-toc__link--active');
        }
      });
    }

    // Set the first item active on load
    if (sections[0]) {
      setActive(sections[0].id);
    }

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      function (entries) {
        entries.forEach(function (entry) {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            setActive(entry.target.id);
          }
        });
      },
      {
        rootMargin: '-10% 0px -75% 0px',
        threshold: 0,
      }
    );

    sections.forEach(function (section) {
      observer.observe(section);
    });
  })();
</script>
